  "output": "Identify any missing information needed to proceed with the workflow setup.Here's what's missing to turn the placeholder into a working workflow, grouped by priority:\n\n**Blocking — must decide before building**\n- **Target format/runtime**: Is this YAML a documentation artifact, or should it become an executable workflow (e.g., a registered dynamic workflow built via the workflow engine, a CI pipeline, or input for a specific tool)? This determines the schema and everything downstream.\n- **Actual process steps**: The five placeholder steps are generic guesses. The real sequence of onboarding stages for this client/organization needs to be defined.\n- **Trigger**: What starts the workflow — signed contract, CRM status change, form submission, manual start?\n\n**Important — needed for correct behavior**\n- **Step owners/roles**: Who is responsible for each step, who approves, who can start the workflow.\n- **Inputs/outputs & dependencies**: What data each step consumes and produces; whether steps run sequentially or in parallel; any branching (e.g., enterprise vs. SMB path).\n- **Client data model**: Which fields are collected (contact info, billing details, technical requirements), and where they're stored.\n- **Integrations**: CRM, billing, e-signature, ticketing, email/slack notifications — which tools, and whether API access/credentials exist.\n\n**Useful — needed for reliability**\n- **SLAs/durations** per step and escalation path when a step stalls.\n- **Error handling**: What happens on failure or rejection at an approval step.\n- **Definition of done**: What marks onboarding complete (account provisioned? kickoff held? first deliverable accepted?).\n- **Templates/artifacts**: Contract templates, welcome packet, kickoff agenda to attach or reference.\n\nThe single most consequential unknown is the target runtime — it dictates whether we refine this YAML or build an executable workflow instead.",
